---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  active="About"
  description="Biography of Andrew Reeves, professor of political science at Washington University in St. Louis."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Biography"
    title="About Andrew Reeves"
    intro="A scholar of presidential power, public policy, and democratic accountability."
    compact
  />

  <section class="container-wide page-section">
    <figure class="about-profile-photo mx-auto mb-10 max-w-5xl overflow-hidden md:mb-14">
      <img
        class="block h-auto w-full"
        src="/images/andrew-reeves-frick-initiatives-speaking.jpg"
        alt="Andrew Reeves speaking at the launch of the Frick Initiatives"
        width="2200"
        height="1466"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>Photo: Suzy Gorman.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <article class="editorial-prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <p>
        Andrew Reeves is Professor of Political Science at Washington University in St. Louis. He studies the American presidency, public policy, and democratic accountability. His work examines how presidents exercise power, how government distributes resources, and how citizens&rsquo; experiences of policy shape their evaluations of elected leaders. He is particularly interested in the geographic and political inequalities that emerge when public policy is put into practice.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reeves&rsquo;s books challenge prevailing accounts of presidential power. <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/no-blank-check/0FE4E2FC0D017DC70566FDFE94B89007"><em>No Blank Check</em></a> demonstrates that Americans care not only about what presidents accomplish but also about how they exercise their authority. <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/particularistic-president/1C2686B436BCBFAB3D46EDBD7C2A17C3"><em>The Particularistic President</em></a>, winner of the 2016 Richard E. Neustadt Award, shows how partisan and electoral incentives shape the distribution of federal resources. His scholarship has appeared in the <em>American Political Science Review</em>, <em>American Journal of Political Science</em>, and <em>Journal of Politics</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reeves directs the <a href="https://wc.wustl.edu/">Weidenbaum Center</a>, a hub for interdisciplinary social science at WashU. The center supports ambitious scholarship, builds research infrastructure, convenes conversations on pressing public issues, and connects scholars and their ideas with policymakers, civic leaders, and the broader public.
      </p>

      <p>
        He also directs the <a href="https://frick.washu.edu/">Frick Initiatives</a>, a civic venture that invests in &ldquo;ideas that travel.&rdquo; The initiatives support programs and partnerships that translate ideas into action, bring diverse audiences together, and develop promising projects across the university and in the broader community.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reeves also serves as Senior Advisor to the Chancellor and as a Visiting Fellow at the Hoover Institution. He received his PhD from the Department of Government at Harvard University.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
